To get OpenDUNE running on Atari ST/STE, TT or Falcon:


* Building

cross compile using cross mint and vasm (vasmm68k_mot):
http://vincent.riviere.free.fr/soft/m68k-atari-mint/
http://sun.hasenbraten.de/vasm/

or download a pre-configured toolchain from:
https://github.com/sidecartridge/atarist-toolkit-docker


for the default 68030 version use :

./configure --host=m68k-atari-mint --os=TOS

to specify the CPU type (68000, 68010, 68020, 68030, 68040 or
68060) add --cpu :

./configure --host=m68k-atari-mint --os=TOS --cpu=68000

The 68030 build will not run on a stock ST/STE. To build a version
that will run on any machine in the Atari ST family, use --cpu=68000.

The opt-in ST/STE supervisor-resident mode enters supervisor mode once
with Super(0L), then continues initialization/gameplay in that mode.
Enable it with --CFLAGS="-DATARI_SUPERVISOR_RESIDENT=1" when configuring;
the default is 0, retaining the Supexec baseline. TT/Falcon and other
_MCH families retain Supexec even in the enabled build; no _MCH cookie
is treated as plain ST. This mode is independent of blitter experiments.
Video-base updates, Mega STE speed changes and IKBD installation/restoration
call their protected routines directly while resident. STE DMA sound start
also has a direct assembly entry, and stop/status polling avoid their
XBIOS trampolines.

Residency does not permanently mask interrupts. IKBD setup/teardown preserve
the caller's status register instead of leaving a fixed interrupt mask.
If startup switches from user mode, it registers an atexit handler before
switching, restoring the saved supervisor stack with mintlib's SuperToUser
binding after normal game cleanup and before the existing exit keypress
prompt. Early returns and exit() paths also run that handler. An
already-supervisor caller is left in its original mode. Other
BIOS/XBIOS/GEMDOS calls remain unchanged.

Compare identical stabilized replays against the Supexec baseline, and check
cursor, audio, error exits and normal desktop return on target. The
supervisor mode is experimental; host/object checks cannot establish
OS-stack behavior or real-hardware correctness. This changes
privilege/dispatch only, not viewport publication order or cursor compositing.

Reference: Vanilla-Conquer-Atari-ST, commit
2ec08811ca7b24a365922c29be7a7c3e727c883c,
tiberiandawn/atarilib/startup.cpp:212-218, keeps the executable in supervisor
mode after one Super(0L); it does not pass the game loop to Supexec.


* Performance

The engine announcement banner supports this opendune.ini setting:

[opendune]
phase_announcement=2
phase_credits=2

Use 0 for immediate display without scrolling, 1 for the original one-row
animation (default), or 2 for two-row steps. Message priorities, queuing and
display hold times are unchanged; only the transition animation changes.
The setting is handled by the engine animator, not the Atari video driver,
and is available on other platforms too. Invalid values warn and use 1.
For credits, 0 keeps normal gradual counting but displays non-scrolling
digits only when the shown number changes, 1 retains the original animation,
and 2 scrolls in two-row steps instead of skipping alternate animation calls.
The engine resolves the visual number pair and scroll offset before renderer
setup; unchanged visual states do not compose or publish. Counting is unchanged
in every mode. ST/STE clicks follow published whole-credit changes; other
machines retain the original sound timing. Forced/reset draws and the final settled
value are always displayed, even in mode 2.
These settings are independent and neither changes game speed.

Gameplay viewport entry/return defaults to immediate display on ST/STE.
The original randomized 8x2-pixel dissolve can be restored with:

[opendune]
viewport_fade=1

Use 0 to disable the dissolve explicitly. This engine-level policy covers
scenario entry and returns from Mentat and Factory screens. The viewport is
still redrawn normally; it only skips the dissolve, not asset loading or
cold-cache work. Other screen transitions are unchanged. The default remains
enabled on TT/Falcon and non-Atari platforms. Invalid values warn and use the
platform default.

Based on a typical scene on level 1 :

- 68000 build on ST/STE is 6-15fps (busy-quiet), Mega STE is 18fps
- 68030 build is 60fps (the maximum frame rate)

Press F8 to toggle the FPS counter. It shows integer video updates per
second, refreshed approximately once a second using the TOS 200 Hz clock.
The elapsed interval is measured, so slow frames and stalls are included.
Updates with no dirty game pixels still count; this is not a count of
distinct game images or CRT refreshes. Enabling it starts a fresh sample,
showing zero until the first measurement is available.

On ST/STE, the building-placement preview is drawn directly in planar
memory, above the scene and below the mouse cursor. Moving it does not
invalidate terrain or trigger chunky-to-planar conversion by itself.
The footprint stays tile-aligned (up to 3x3 tiles), with an X for invalid
locations, and is clipped to the battlefield. Ordinary selected-building
outlines retain their original rendering. Options and modal messages
hide the preview until gameplay drawing resumes.

ST/STE battlefield terrain and structure tiles use permanent planar cache
slots, decoded on first use and restored directly to visible screen memory.
Tile mask opacity (empty/mixed/opaque) is cached in the existing planar
slot status byte during lazy decoding. CPU publication selects a proven
opaque ground or overlay/fog source without rescanning static masks,
skipping mask reads and ground/fog composition for that source.
Mixed images retain their mask-based composition, and cursor/placement
backup maintenance remains unchanged.
Aligned 15x10 viewport tile writes test independent cursor and placement
overlap masks (ten words each), updated only when their actual planar
backups are drawn or erased. Cursor marking includes all saved 16-pixel
groups, even transparent padding; placement marking retains its drawn
rectangle. Requested movement/hiding alone does not clear a live backup's
mask. Tiles outside this grid and sprite/other rectangle writes retain
the existing overlap checks. ST/STE tiles and overlays share the same
logical screen; hardware screen shake does not change that drawing base.
The generic rectangle-overlap wrapper is inline for other planar publishers.
House-colored tiles have six cached variants; unaffected tiles share one
image. The gameplay IBM palette and quantization are fixed, so hardware
fades/dimming need no tile-cache rebuild. Tile decoding uses private buffers
and never writes to the visible screen as scratch.

Ordinary battlefield sprites are decoded into small private buffers and
converted with explicit silhouette masks; no final background c2p sweep is
needed for these scenes. This experimental branch adds two 64-entry planar
sprite image caches, roughly 243 KiB together, to the planarcredits milestone.
Eligible frames are decoded and converted through the shared c2p encoder
on first use, then drawn directly from cached plane words and opacity masks.
GUI_DrawSprite takes the final sprite/frame ID and colour-house identity
alongside its existing arguments. Embedded palettes have a separate identity;
unknown assets or arbitrary palettes retain the general renderer. Internally,
a packed five-word prescription includes the body's frame, colour-house
variant, both flips, canonical highlight and resolved X phase (x & 15),
plus up to four ordered layers, each with its frame, colour-house variant,
flips and signed offsets. An absent selection layer distinguishes unselected
variants. Embedded-palette and direct palette-index sprites are supported.
Ground units submit body, harvesting attachment, turret, smoke and selection
to one GUI_DrawSprite call. Individual components are cached independently
by frame, house, flips and highlight, without position or X phase. Only a
component miss decodes into private chunky memory and uses the shared c2p
encoder. Composite misses merge cached planar components and opacity masks
in the original layer order, aligning them in private planar memory.
Adding selection or smoke, moving a turret, or changing X phase reuses the
other components without decoding or c2p. Composite eviction does not evict
components; component eviction does not invalidate completed composites.
Hits publish the complete unit in one overlay-safe blit; the selection marker
is not drawn separately. Entries accommodate aligned composites up to 80x64.
Each tier uses the same flat-array lookup and global least-recently-used
replacement as atari-st-planar-present, with integer-key comparisons and
independent 16-bit usage clocks resetting timestamps on wrap.
Aligned/flipped planar
pixels and opacity masks are generated on demand; cache hits do not decode,
compose palettes, perform c2p or shift plane/mask words. Clipping stays
at publication time; absolute position and Y phase are not part of the key.
This is internal image caching, without command collection, patch staging
or damage histories.
Sprite reloads release the cache. IBM.PAL, the 16 hardware pens and
quantization are static throughout sprite life; house ID is the only
palette variant in the prescription. Cache lookup does not inspect palette
contents or poll palette generations.
Allocation failure logs a warning and retains the original sprite path.
Viewport tile and sprite writes that overlap only the mouse cursor use normal
copy/transparency loops, then immediately refresh the cursor backup and redraw
only the overwritten cursor pixels. Repair is restricted to the publication's
intersection with the drawn cursor and honours its actual write masks, including
transparent holes and clipping. Neighbouring tiles repair their own intersections
when published; repairs are not deferred to the end of the viewport pass.
Cursor-overlap publication is kept out of line. The ordinary cached-sprite
copy loop is isolated from cursor repair state to avoid 68000 register spills.
Placement-preview overlaps and outside-viewport/UI writes retain the existing
protected merge path. Cursor movement still restores the saved background.
Unsupported effects and non-planar destinations retain separate layer draws
in their original order. Terrain and units still write visibly in painter order:
this may shorten sprite invisibility, but does not eliminate the exposed
erase/redraw interval or guarantee tear-free output.

Partial fog edges are combined with the cached
ground tile before each planar write, so covered terrain is never briefly
exposed by a separate ground-then-fog blit. Clear terrain retains its
unmasked copy path. Damaged fully hidden cells restore an opaque cached
colour-12 tile, including viewport-only damage from neighbouring sprite
footprints. They join the same repair-on-first-touch pass as other terrain:
each tile is repaired once, before current foreground publication or in the
final sweep, so old traces disappear without erasing current sprites.
Unchanged hidden cells are not redrawn. The fog tile adds 160 bytes of
pixels/masks and is encoded once per tile-cache load through the shared
fixed gameplay palette mapping; subsequent repairs need no c2p conversion.
Aircraft shadows are omitted on ST/STE, including its
chunky fallbacks, so a flyover no longer disables cached planar rendering
for the whole viewport. Aircraft bodies retain their normal colours and
painter order; other platforms retain their shadows. Visible sandworm blur
and blurred units retain the original chunky compositor because they read
underlying logical palette indices. Switching paths reconstructs the complete scene.
ST/STE unit-to-target and target-to-unit selection changes reset widget state
without redrawing unchanged base widgets or running the second full widget
draw pass. Changed widget visibility/selection still triggers drawing, and
the action panel is rebuilt normally for command text, Cancel, and unit
controls. Other selection transitions and TT/Falcon keep the original path.
Attack, Move and Harvest panels also share six small planar cache slots
(about 15.4 KiB). A miss renders in SCREEN_1, then the shared c2p encoder
reads its 320-byte-stride rows straight into the packed slot. Hits restore
the slot with cursor-safe planar publication, without rendering, c2p or
a chunky SCREEN_0 write. Keys include command, Cancel selection/hover,
language and palette mapping generation. Widget state and font setup
still update on hits; SCREEN_1 is scratch, not a maintained panel mirror.
Normal Cancel hover/press redraws retain their existing small-button path.
Other panels and unsupported layouts keep their original renderer.
Off-screen composition and viewport fades also retain the original path.
The planar viewport defers dirty terrain publication until the first
foreground draw touching each tile, then draws that foreground immediately.
Each dirty tile is repaired once; remaining tiles are repaired before
aircraft are drawn. Existing damage estimates and redraw eligibility are
unchanged, with no command queue or new protection masks. See
ATARI_PHASE_REVERSAL.md for the experiment and possible later damage tightening.
After direct planar rendering, conservative summaries skip battlefield
dirty-mask clearing for arrays with no possible pending battlefield bits.
Each marked array gets a constant-mask pass, combined when both are
marked; partial rectangle clears leave its summary set until a battlefield
or full-screen clear. Top rows and sidebar bits, including the first
sidebar column and minimap, are preserved. Chunky fallback scenes retain
their pending conversion work.
The planar tile cache fills tiles lazily at their existing
draw entry points. Tile/house indices and permanent pixel slots retain the
previous layout and sharing of non-recoloured tiles; a ready byte per slot
is the only hit check after the direct index lookup. The small hot lookup
is inline; mask construction and decoding live in a separate cold-fill
function so hits do not enter its decoding stack frame. There is no eviction:
cached pixel addresses remain valid until the tile assets are freed.
Load-time setup still scans for house variants, but decodes no planar
tiles. The separate chunky cache and its eager preparation are unchanged.

On a miss, packed pixels and the house-adjusted tile palette pass through
the shared c2p encoder directly into the final cache slot. Tiles have
compact native ST rows (8 bytes, four interleaved plane words), 128 bytes
per image, with separate 32-byte masks. A cold fill prepares an aligned
256-byte chunky tile and converts all 16 rows directly into the slot in
one call, without padding caches or copying completed planar tiles.
The stride-aware c2p entry accepts separate source/destination byte
strides (16/8 for tiles). Compact sprite components and private window
canvases use the same batched encoder. Full-width screen conversion and
single-row work retain the original fixed-stride entry. Both assembly
entries expand one shared pixel kernel; the existing fixed screen entry
remains byte-for-byte unchanged. Strides are even, at least the source/
destination row widths, and the source stride must fit 16 bits.
The fixed IBM.PAL mapping is initialized once at tile load; cold fills
never use a displayed loading/fade palette or alter palette state.

The standard assets still reserve about 104 KiB for house-aware tiles,
plus one ready byte per planar slot. A 256-byte gameplay pen map and
8 KiB restricted pair lookup replace the former temporary 128 KiB tile
lookup; only 256 pair entries need initialization. This is a lazy-fill,
stable-address cache, not a bounded working-set cache. Sprite masks/cache
metadata add about 29 KiB, separately from the sprite image caches above.
Allocation failures log a warning and retain the original renderer.
TT/Falcon rendering is unchanged. Startup and first-draw timings need
measurement on the target.

Click-to-center actions on ST/STE (minimap, viewport right-click, and the
selected-object name) reuse the same incremental planar shift as edge
scrolling while any viewport overlap remains. Diagonal shifts clear all
exposed corners as well as the new rows and columns. Jumps with no overlap
rebuild the viewport.

The upper announcement banner converts its 24-row text composition once
per slide on ST/STE, then copies the moving 14-row planar window without
further c2p. Its 3.75 KiB cache uses the fixed gameplay mapping; masked
horizontal edges preserve the adjacent HUD, and cursor backgrounds are
updated through the normal planar helpers. Timing and text queuing are
unchanged.

Atari credits numbers use a small six-column unsigned formatter instead
of general printf machinery, with no division or stream handling. Equal
old/new values share one formatted result. Leading spaces, decimal
carries and digit scrolling are unchanged.

ST/STE credits use a short YM2149 channel-C tone instead of loading an
XMIDI effect for each tick: about 1.95 kHz when increasing, 1.56 kHz when
decreasing. Ticks accompany published whole-credit changes, not intermediate
scroll rows or unseen counting updates. Force/reset draws establish the
displayed value silently. Sound settings are respected; other machines and
other effects retain MIDI.
Dosound runs the tone asynchronously with one 50 Hz wait command, followed
by explicit volume-zero and register restoration. This is an OS-timed short
pulse of about 40 ms on stock TOS and EmuTOS, not an exact 20 ms duration:
one interpreter interrupt decrements the delay and the following one mutes.
Busy requests are dropped, without a backlog, until the OS sound list
finishes; an existing list or audible channel-C tone is not interrupted.
Other channels and PSG I/O mixer bits are preserved. Sound stop/shutdown
also cancels an owned list and explicitly mutes the tone. No busy wait or
framerate limit is introduced.

The standard ST/STE credits counter retains its composed image in a private
288-byte, 64x9 planar buffer. Only affected digits are recomposed; stable
digits are not redrawn when another digit scrolls. Only affected aligned
16-pixel groups are published, using the retained image's 32-byte row stride.
A units-only change publishes 72 bytes instead of the full 288 bytes and
preserves the neighboring tens digit even though it shares the copied group.
Force/reset draws, cache refreshes, destination changes and returns from a
fallback rebuild and publish the full counter. Changing digits
select nine visible rows from the lower/higher glyph pair using the shared
scrolling phase; unchanged digits stay fixed. No per-update c2p or
horizontal glyph shifting is needed. The background and six-position
glyph cache add about 8.6 KiB and rebuild when quantized pen assignments
change or sprites reload. Cursor/placement backgrounds are preserved.
Unaligned positions retain the chunky cache; unsupported assets, widget
sizes and other machines retain their existing rendering paths.

Minimap 2x2 and 3x3 icons are decoded once on ST/STE into a 336-byte
logical-pixel cache. Opaque icons use four or nine direct SCREEN_1 stores;
transparent icons retain their exact opacity. Radar classification
and logical pixel colours are preserved.
Unsupported icons and other machines retain the generic sprite renderer.

Pending minimap tile notifications are coalesced using the existing dirty
bitset. Repeated notifications before a tile is processed take only one
queue entry and one preparation pass. No extra memory is needed.

ST/STE also remembers each tile's last rendered minimap icon or logical
pixel colour in a 4.5 KiB appearance cache. Unchanged appearances skip
pixel writes and do not expand the rows published through c2p. Terrain
and object classification still runs, so actual radar changes remain
immediate. Full reconstruction, scenario/scale changes and icon-cache
reloads invalidate the cache; moving the white viewport outline forces
restoration of its previous tiles. Single changed rows are published too.
Other machines retain their original tile rendering.

Ordinary ST/STE minimap batches run at most twice per second, using a
30-tick interval on the 60 Hz GUI clock. Notifications remain pending
and coalesce between batches. If the 200-entry queue fills, the next
batch drains the complete pending bitset rather than delaying overflow
tiles over additional intervals. Viewport-outline movement and explicit
radar/interface reconstruction remain immediate. Other machines retain
their original update cadence.

* Precompiled versions

http://nanard.free.fr/opendune/

Discussion about the port on atari-forum.com :
http://www.atari-forum.com/viewtopic.php?f=26&t=29873


* Running

- MIDI music is sent to the ATARI MIDI Out port : use a General MIDI
or MT32 compatible expander to hear music. Set mt32midi=1 in OPENDUNE.INI
to enable the MT32 songs.

- DMA sound samples are converted once when loaded and retained in explicit
ST-RAM allocations. Playback reads their PCM payloads directly, without
per-play sample copies. This includes word-aligned starts and silent padding
for odd sample lengths. The 32 KB conversion scratch also supplies on-demand
speech; it is not a staging copy for resident effects.

- 320x200 (RGB) or 320x240 (VGA) 8bpp modes are used on Falcon.
TT Low (320x480 8bpp) is used on TT.
ST Low (320x200 4bpp) is used on ST/STE.

- Explosion screen shake on ST/STE changes the hardware screen base,
without a full-screen c2p redraw or an extra screen allocation. The image
moves down 4 lines on STE/Mega STE, or 8 lines on ST for its coarser
address alignment. The exposed top rows read memory before the existing
TOS screen and may show garbage; no padding is reserved or cleared.

- on a Mega STE the CPU is switched to 16MHz with the cache enabled
at startup, and put back to whatever it was on exit. Detected from the
_MCH cookie, so nothing is touched on any other machine.

- Keyboard and mouse input is managed with a custom IKBD interrupt
handler.

- Digitized Sound and Voices are played using STE DMA Sound.
Depending on your Falcon configuration, you may need to run
FPATCH.PRG / FPATCH2.PRG before launching the game

- OPENDUNE.INI file should be located in the same directory as
OPENDUNE.TOS.

- DOS Dune II v1.07 original files should be in a DATA subdirectory,
savegames and options will be stored in a SAVES subdirectory.


Thomas Bernard


* Added by Neil Rackett (neilrackett.com), 2026 :

- Support for 68000 and other CPUs (--cpu build option)
- Automatic 16MHz+cache switching on Mega STE
- Fixes for misaligned word accesses
- Fixes for cutscene timing on slower machines
- Build fix for current MiNTLib (VsetScreen)
